<p>The fifteen-day ceiling on police custody survives. What moved is the window within which those days can be taken. Under the CrPC, an investigating officer who could not justify police remand in the first fortnight lost it for good. The BNSS text permits the fifteen days to be claimed in instalments spread across the early weeks of the investigation.</p>

<h2>The remand timeline</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Arrest.</strong> Grounds of arrest must be communicated — in writing, per the Supreme Court&rsquo;s recent jurisprudence — and the arrest memo prepared.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Production within 24 hours.</strong> Article 22(2) and Section 58 BNSS require production before the nearest Magistrate within twenty-four hours, excluding journey time.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>First remand.</strong> The Magistrate may authorise detention — police or judicial custody — recording reasons. Police custody is the exception and needs case-specific justification.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The custody window.</strong> Up to fifteen days of police custody in aggregate, available within the first forty or sixty days depending on the offence.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Default bail point.</strong> Sixty or ninety days without a chargesheet matures the indefeasible right under Section 187(3) BNSS, if exercised before the chargesheet is filed.</div>
</div>

<h2>What the change means for bail practice</h2>
<p>Three practical consequences follow. First, the exposure to police remand no longer ends at day fifteen, so counsel opposing a belated police-custody application must engage with the application&rsquo;s substance — why custody, why now, what interrogation remains — rather than relying on the old temporal bar alone. Second, the change interacts with bail timing: an accused enlarged on bail is beyond remand, so the realistic prospect of a later custody application is itself a factor pressing for early, well-prepared bail applications. Third, the default bail clock is unaffected; the sixty and ninety day arithmetic, and the discipline of applying before the chargesheet arrives, carry over intact from the Section 167 jurisprudence.</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>A point to watch:</strong> how the forty and sixty day windows operate in particular scenarios is still being shaped case by case in the High Courts. Where the point is outcome-determinative, the safe course is to argue both the BNSS text and the protective principles underlying the fifteen-day jurisprudence, and to keep the record precise on every remand date.</p>
</div>

<h2>The checklist at a remand hearing</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Verify the arrest time and the twenty-four-hour computation from actual detention.</p>
  <p>Demand and record the written grounds of arrest; note any defect immediately.</p>
  <p>Count the custody days already taken, police and judicial, with dates.</p>
  <p>Oppose police custody on necessity: what specific investigative step requires custodial presence.</p>
  <p>Diarise the sixty or ninety day default-bail date from the first remand, and prepare the application in advance.</p>
</div>

<h2>The wider frame</h2>
<p>Remand law is where personal liberty meets investigative need at its sharpest point, and every structural change to it redistributes leverage. The BNSS&rsquo;s reworking of the custody window is among the most consequential of the 2023 codes&rsquo; procedural changes for the arrested person. Counsel who still reason from the Section 167 reflexes — and prosecutors who overread the new text — will both find the ground has shifted. The dates, the grounds of arrest, and the custody arithmetic now decide more than they ever did.</p>
